// Langton's ants, many of them: on white an ant turns right, on black it
// turns left; it flips the colour it leaves and steps on. A cell is two
// layers, its shade and the ant on it, so an ant's step claims the shade it
// flips and the two places it moves between, and nothing else. Two ants
// stepping into one cell clash, and neither goes: in a later group, each
// turns where it stands instead, as does an ant facing an edge or another
// ant. Phib's stress test scatters them on a big white board.

type Shade = White | Black
type Bug = Clear | Ant(face: Dir)
cell Shade / Bug
look White model "grass"
look Black model "grass/trail"
look Ant model "ant"

group 0
recipe right "Ant on white"
  White / Ant(E)  ->  Black / Clear
  _ / Clear       ->  * / Ant(S)
recipe left "Ant on black"
  _ / Clear       ->  * / Ant(N)
  Black / Ant(E)  ->  White / Clear

group 1
recipe blocked "Ant blocked"
  Ant(E)  ->  Ant(S)

legend . = White / Clear
legend n = White / Ant(N)
legend e = White / Ant(E)
legend s = White / Ant(S)
legend w = White / Ant(W)
